title: From one wire back to a number
mode: vhdl
chapter: Buses and numbers
check: behaves
---- intro
The decoder turned a number into one wire per value. An **encoder** goes back: exactly one bit of `d` is 1, and `y` is **which** one, as a number.

| d | y |
|---|---|
| 0001 | 00 |
| 0010 | 01 |
| 0100 | 10 |
| 1000 | 11 |

Anything else (no bit on, or several) should give `"00"`.
---- task
Make `y` the number of the bit of `d` that is 1; `"00"` for anything else.
---- start
library ieee;
use ieee.std_logic_1164.all;

entity encoder is
  port(
    d : in  std_logic_vector(3 downto 0);
    y : out std_logic_vector(1 downto 0)
  );
end encoder;

architecture rtl of encoder is
begin
    -- write it here
end rtl;
---- answer
library ieee;
use ieee.std_logic_1164.all;

entity encoder is
  port(
    d : in  std_logic_vector(3 downto 0);
    y : out std_logic_vector(1 downto 0)
  );
end encoder;

architecture rtl of encoder is
begin
    with d select y <=
        "00" when "0001",
        "01" when "0010",
        "10" when "0100",
        "11" when "1000",
        "00" when others;
end rtl;
---- hint1
Use `with d select`: four cases from the table, and `when others` for the rest.
---- hint2
The last line is `"00" when others;`
---- solution
```
with d select y <=
    "00" when "0001",
    "01" when "0010",
    "10" when "0100",
    "11" when "1000",
    "00" when others;
```
---- done
A keyboard uses an encoder: one key down becomes the number of that key.
